                </div>
            </div> <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">This postcard image was popular in France in the early 1920s. It shows a well-dressed man, perhaps English or American, listening to a phonograph record playing holiday greetings and good wishes in both French and English. The artist, "Right.", drew many humorous postcards in the same style as this.</note>
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ adds four handwritten lines on the image-side of this postcard expressing Christmas greetings in Greek, Italian, German, and English (see section “Images” for a reproduction of the postcard). The phrase “κάλα Χριστóυγενα” is JJ’s rendition of “Merry Christmas” in modern Greek. His idiosyncratic spelling for the Greek is retained. κάλα means “good, noble, fine”, and -γεννα refers to “genesis” – “Good Birth of Christ”. Thanks to Stephanie Nelson for assistance with the Greek. </note>
            </div>
